    "K_t": "Evidence:\n[1] There are many types of trellis for different places and for different plants, from agricultural types, especially in viticulture, which are covered at vine training systems, to garden uses for climbers such as grapevines, clematis, ivy, and climbing roses or other support based growing plants.\n[2] They are considered fairly easy to grow and they may produce large quantities of seeds that germinate freely around the parent plant.\n[3] The relationships between Burretiokentia and some other genera of the tribe Basseliniinae including \"Physokentia\" and the New Caledonia endemic \"Cyphophoenix\" are not clear.\n[4] The male and female flower types look the same externally; the stamens are reduced in female plants and the stigmas are reduced in the male."
